example:
```json
{"messages": [
  {"role": "user", "content": "Summarise ticket #8812: <ticket text>"},
  {"role": "assistant", "content": "Area: billing. Issue: double charge on 3 Oct. Action: refund issued."}
]}
```

About 2,000 lines like this (JSONL), and the model reliably answers in exactly that format.

```json
{"id": "refund-07", "input": "Can I get a refund on my annual plan after 10 days?", "expected": "Yes — full refund within 14 days", "source": "policy.md"}
{"id": "billing-12", "input": "Can I move my billing date to the 1st?", "expected": "NOT_COVERED"}
```

~150 lines like these, reviewed by a person, versioned next to the prompt and run on every change.

```json
// You declare: get_order_status(order_id: string)
// The model replies with a call instead of prose:
{"type": "tool_use", "name": "get_order_status", "input": {"order_id": "8812"}}
// Your code runs it and returns the result:
{"type": "tool_result", "content": "shipped 2026-10-09, arriving 2026-10-14"}
// The model answers: "Order 8812 shipped on 9 Oct and should arrive on the 14th."
```

```json
// In any MCP client's config: start a server, and its tools appear to the model
{"mcpServers": {
  "github": {"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"]}
}}
// The client now lists tools like search_code and create_issue — no bespoke integration code
```

